# Binary Converter

A small C++ command-line utility for converting between decimal numbers and binary representations.

## Features

- Convert a decimal integer to a 32-bit binary representation.
- Accept positive and negative integer values.
- Validate numeric input and report invalid characters or duplicate decimal points.
- Provide a binary-to-number conversion routine in the source code.

## Requirements

- A C++ compiler available as `c++`
- GNU Make
- Git, if cloning the project from GitHub

## Build

Clone the repository and build the executable:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/CodeAndCofe/btn-ntb.git
cd btn-ntb
make
```

This creates an executable named `binary`.

## Usage

The program expects one argument. The first character selects the conversion mode:

- `n` — treat the remaining characters as a decimal number and convert it to binary.
- `b` — treat the remaining characters as a binary value and convert it to a number.

### Decimal to binary

```bash
./binary n13
```

Example output:

```text
00000000 00000000 00000000 00001101
```

Negative numbers can be passed with a minus sign:

```bash
./binary n-13
```

### Binary to decimal

```bash
./binary b1101
```

> **Note:** The binary-to-decimal conversion routine is present in the current implementation, but the command-line entry point does not yet call `output_number()`. As a result, this mode currently performs the conversion without displaying the result. Calling `output_number()` after `convert_to_number()` in `converter.cpp` will print the converted number.

## Makefile Commands

```bash
make          # Build the binary executable
make clean    # Remove object files
make fclean   # Remove object files and the executable
```

## Project Structure

- `converter.cpp` — command-line entry point and mode selection.
- `numbers.cpp` — decimal-to-binary conversion and binary output.
- `conver_to_number.cpp` — binary-to-decimal conversion logic.
- `Converter.hpp` — `numbers` class declaration.
- `Makefile` — build configuration.

## Input Format

Use the following format:

```text
./binary [n|b]<value>
```

For example:

```bash
./binary n42
./binary b101010
```

